  "narrative": "Compared to the last sale: Current FOB trades of feeder steers and heifers lightly tested but few sold 8.00-12.00 higher. Not enough comparable calf trades to test trends. Demand moderate to good. Seller interest moderate. Supplies becoming lighter as we head into the holiday season. Cattle futures remain extremely volatile and has everyone guessing. Timely rains has turned wheat pasture conditions around in the midwest and this has improve demand for calves. Supply included: 59 percent from the South Central region, 18 percent from the North Central region, 14 percent from the South East, 8 percent from the West region, and <1 percent for the Northeastern region. Current delivery is 14 day for the day of the sale and is up to and including December 3, 2026. Deliveries are current through February 2027. Supply included: 95% Feeder Cattle (50% Steers, 3% Dairy Steers, 30% Heifers, 8% Beef/Dairy Steers, 9% Beef/Dairy Heifers); 5% Replacement Cattle (36% Bred Cows, 60% Bred Heifers, 3% Cow-Calf Pairs, 1% Heifer Pairs). Feeder cattle over 600 lbs was 63%. Feeder Cattle prices FOB based on net weights after a 0-3% pencil shrink or equivalent, with up to a 40 cent slide > 600 lbs and up to a 50 cent slide < 600 lbs. Some cattle sold with the Superior Right Slide and were slid .90-1.40 per pound.",
